📌 Customer Churn EDA Summary

This project performs Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) on the Telco Customer Churn dataset to understand customer behavior and identify factors influencing churn.

✅ 1. Churn Overview

Total customers: 7043

Churned customers: 1869

Non-churn customers: 5174

Churn rate: ~26.5%

Insight:
Churn is a real business problem and needs attention. The dataset is imbalanced with more non-churn customers.

📊 2. Churn Distribution
Figure: Count of Customers by Churn

No (Not churned): 5174

Yes (Churned): 1869

Conclusion:
Most customers stay with the company, but the churn percentage is significant.

👵 3. Senior Citizen Distribution
Figure: Total number of senior citizens

No: 83.8%

Yes: 16.2%

Conclusion:
Senior citizens are a small segment but may have higher churn rates. Special strategies may be required to retain them.

🔍 4. Key EDA Findings
Churn by Gender

Gender has no significant impact on churn.

Churn by Senior Citizen

Senior citizens show higher churn rate compared to non-senior customers.

Tenure vs Churn

Customers with short tenure churn more.

Long-tenure customers are more loyal.

Contract Type vs Churn

Month-to-month contract has highest churn.

1-year and 2-year contracts show lower churn.

Service Features vs Churn

Fiber optic users churn more.

Customers with security, backup, and tech support services churn less.

Billing & Payment Method

Paperless billing customers churn more.

Electronic check payment shows highest churn.

Automatic payments show lowest churn.

💰 5. Charges vs Churn
Monthly Charges

Low charges (20–30) → less churn

High charges (70–100) → more churn

Insight:
Higher monthly bills increase churn risk due to price sensitivity.

Total Charges

Low total charges (0–500) → high churn (new customers)

High total charges (>3000) → low churn (long-tenure customers)

Insight:
Churn mostly happens in early months.

🎯 Final Conclusion
High-risk churn profile

Customers most likely to churn are:

New customers (low total charges)

High monthly charges

Month-to-month contract

Fiber optic internet

No add-on services (security, backup, support)

Electronic check payment
Overall Conclusion:
Churn occurs early in the customer lifecycle and is strongly linked with high monthly charges and low total charges. Improving onboarding, offering long-term contracts, and promoting value-added services can significantly reduce churn.